## Run

```bash
python -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate
pip install -r requirements.txt
cp .env.example .env
python wsgi.py                 # or: flask --app wsgi run --port 4200
```

There is **no migration step**. The schema is created automatically at startup by
`db.create_all()` (see `app/__init__.py` → `app/generated/db.py::init_db`), which reads the
generated SQLAlchemy models and issues `CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS` for each. That is ideal for
development and demos with the default SQLite database.

### Production schema evolution

`create_all()` never alters existing tables. For real schema migrations, add
[Flask-Migrate](https://flask-migrate.readthedocs.io/) (Alembic): uncomment it in
`requirements.txt`, initialise it against `app.generated.db.db`, then use
`flask db migrate` / `flask db upgrade` and remove the `init_db(app)` call from the factory.

## Layout

```
wsgi.py                   # WSGI entry point — YOURS (written once)
app/
  __init__.py             # create_app() factory — YOURS (written once)
  generated/              # owned by Backbone — regenerated wholesale, do not edit
    db.py                 # the SQLAlchemy() instance + init_db()
    api.py                # register_blueprints(app)
    models/               # SQLAlchemy models (+ to_dict / apply_json)
    schemas/              # manual camelCase validation (validate_create/update)
    services/             # CRUD business layer over db.session
    routes/               # Flask blueprints (only detected endpoints)
<% if (auth) { %>    auth/                 # register / login / me + require_auth decorator
<% } %>```

Everything under `app/generated/` is overwritten on regenerate. `wsgi.py` and `app/__init__.py`
are yours to edit.

